tgs:Monologue.Frankly_135_OilOneOhOne a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:createdDate "2026-09-10"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:updatedDate "2026-09-10"^^xsd:date ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Untold Energy Story: What You Actually Need to Know About Oil"@en ;
    skos:definition "Nate directly opens by naming the real, urgent occasion for this episode: with his team's own longer, professionally-produced 'Reality 101' series still in production, the real, unfolding Strait of Hormuz crisis prompted him to rush-produce this three-part video series on oil in three days. The first installment covers real, foundational concepts — what oil physically is, the real, direct work it performs, and why most people remain 'energy blind' to it."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-03-30"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-09"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/135-oil-101> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uRIxJ4B3fNQ> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Frankly-135-Transcript-Oil-101.docx.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_136_OilTwoOhOne, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_77_SevenPhilosophiesOnTheFuture ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly reframes oil's own real origin story away from the popular 'dinosaur goo' image: it formed from ancient marine phytoplankton compressed over geologic time, a real 'solar battery that took millions of years to charge' and that humanity is draining in centuries. He's real and direct with the precise, quantified math: one barrel of oil contains roughly 1,700 kilowatt-hours of real work potential, equivalent to about 5 years of average human labor once combined with machines, at a real, current market cost of roughly $100 — an economic subsidy he calls 'indistinguishable from magic on human timescales.' Scaling this up, he's direct that roughly 100 billion barrels of oil-equivalent fossil hydrocarbons are consumed globally each year, equivalent to a real 500 billion human-labor-equivalents working alongside an actual 5 billion human workforce — a ratio of 100 fossil 'ghost workers' for every living one. He's real and direct about his own personal, professional experience of energy blindness: his three real, Nobel-Prize-winning graduate economics professors at the University of Chicago never once mentioned energy as a contributor to economic productivity, since the ample supply throughout their own careers let them treat a genuinely exceptional period of history as though it were simply normal. Closes on a real, direct, personal metabolic comparison: the human body requires roughly 2,000 kilocalories daily, while the average American's real, total energy footprint — heating, transport, food, supply chains — runs to roughly 200,000 kilocalories daily, a hundred times the body's own actual need, almost entirely invisible in daily experience."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct first installment of a genuine three-part trilogy, recorded back-to-back with Frankly_136 and Frankly_137 (both already built) and released on consecutive days. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic and institutional (EIA data, Reality Blind citations, real-time Strait of Hormuz news coverage) rather than pointing to further individuals."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_136_OilTwoOhOne a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Built for Cheap Energy: What Happens When the Oil Stops Flowing"@en ;
    skos:definition "The real, direct second installment of the Oil trilogy, extending the first video's own foundational framing into how modern institutions, food systems, and global supply chains are structurally calibrated to cheap, abundant energy — and what genuinely breaks when that assumption fails."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-03-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-10"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/136-oil-201> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_kEwDNlNwF0> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Frankly-136-Transcript-Oil-201.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_135_OilOneOhOne, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_137_OilThreeOhOne, tgs:Interview.TGS_101_ShaleOilAndTheSlurpingSound ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly grounds the food system's own real energy dependence in precise figures: roughly 10 calories of fossil hydrocarbon input go into every single calorie of food reaching a plate, and roughly half the nitrogen in a human body today carries a real, direct chemical signature traceable to the Haber-Bosch industrial process — a single process that alone enables Earth to feed roughly 4 of its 8 billion people. He's real and direct that swapping to electric vehicles addresses only a real, narrow slice of oil's total function: only about 40% of a barrel becomes gasoline, with the remainder feeding roughly 6,000 other real, everyday products, from asphalt to medicines to synthetic clothing. A real, pointed corrective closes his own treatment of corporate blame: only around 12% of global oil reserves belong to publicly traded companies like ExxonMobil or Shell, with the other 88% held by national oil companies — meaning oil is genuinely 'a story of nations and geology, not corporations.' He's real and direct in distinguishing energy from power: organisms and economies alike compete on power (the rate of usable energy delivery), not raw energy quantity, which is why oil's own real, direct combination of density, portability, and storability cannot simply be substituted by intermittent wind and solar without also solving for reliable, on-demand delivery. Closes on real, direct historical correction: contrary to the popular 'energy transition' narrative, humanity has never actually transitioned off a prior energy source, only ever added new ones on top — directly invoking Jevons paradox (efficiency gains expanding total consumption, not shrinking it) as the real, underlying mechanical reason why."@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct second installment of the Oil trilogy — see Frankly_135 (already built) for the real, shared production context. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely encyclopedic and institutional (USDA dairy data, Exxon depletion figures, USGS critical-minerals data) rather than pointing to further individuals; TGS_101 (Art Berman, already built) is directly cross-referenced via its own real 'slurping sound' framing, not a new find."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_137_OilThreeOhOne a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "Everything is About to Change: The World After Cheap Energy"@en ;
    skos:definition "The real, direct third and final installment of the Oil trilogy, framing the entire series through the Carbon Pulse: a one-time inheritance of ancient stored sunlight — real, direct source of the 'fossil sunlight' term — being burned through in a few hundred years, with direct, explicit implications for money, governance, ecology, and human psychology on its downslope."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-03-31"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.EnergySystems, tgs:Subject.EcologyAndEnvironment, tgs:Subject.Geopolitics ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
    thinkr:hasCanonicalSource <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/frankly-original/137-oil-301> ;
    thinkr:hasReplay <https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_Jv09GZ4lmE> ;
    thinkr:hasTranscript <https://www.thegreatsimplification.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/Frankly-137-Transcript-Oil-301.pdf> ;
    thinkr:discusses tgs:Concept.CarbonPulse, tgs:Concept.Superorganism ;
    dct:references tgs:Monologue.Frankly_136_OilTwoOhOne, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_121_WideBoundaryNews, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_23_MordorEconomy, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_97_TheSuperorganismIn7Minutes, tgs:Monologue.Frankly_129_DesperatelySeekingAgency ;
    thinkr:hasDiscussionSummary "Nate directly names crude oil and gas as 'fossil sunlight' — ancient solar energy captured by long-dead phytoplankton, concentrated by geologic time — closing the trilogy's own real, central metaphor. He's real and direct that money is a claim on physical work, not a substitute for it, arguing the entire growth-based financial system, its debt, interest, and repayment structures, is predicated on an assumption of ever-more affordable energy that the Carbon Pulse's own real shape does not actually support going forward. A real, direct historical grounding anchors his own warning about deforestation risk: citing Easter Island, the 19th-century US Eastern Seaboard, and Greece after its 2009 financial crisis as real, concrete precedents for what happens when populations lose access to their primary energy source and turn to wood instead. He's real and direct that the energy crisis and the ecological crisis are not two separate problems but 'two faces of the same predicament' — the Carbon Pulse simultaneously created the ecological damage from burning fossil fuels and the depletion crisis from extracting them too quickly. A real, pointed geopolitical observation closes his own systemic analysis: nations are now choosing sides based on energy access rather than ideology, directly citing China's real, long-term LNG and oil contracts with Qatar, Iran, and Russia as concrete evidence the post-WWII global order, itself built on cheap oil tied to American power, is now genuinely in question. Closes on real, direct personal conviction rather than pure systems analysis: naming connection, purpose, community, and service as 'ancient satisfactions' that predate the Carbon Pulse by hundreds of thousands of years and require no barrel of oil at all, framing the Great Simplification as a real, potential 'species growing up' rather than necessarily a collapse, and previewing a real, direct future series called 'Staying Human.'"@en ;
    skos:scopeNote "Real, direct source of the already-confirmed 'fossil sunlight' citation for tgs:Concept.CrudeOil's own alternate-term entry. Real, direct third installment of the Oil trilogy — see Frankly_135 and Frankly_136 (both already built) for the real, shared production context and Strait of Hormuz occasion. Real, further citable material found but NOT built in this pass: this episode's own citation list is otherwise largely current-events/institutional (real-time 2026 Reuters/UNCTAD coverage of the Hormuz crisis, OPEC data) rather than pointing to further individuals; Frankly_75 ('Somebody's Gonna Win'), directly cited, is not yet built."@en ;
    .


tgs:Monologue.Frankly_138_Part1ChangingTheFuture a thinkr:Episode, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeType thinkr:EpisodeType.Monologue ;
    thinkr:hasEpisodeSegment tgs:EpisodeSegment.Frankly ;
    skos:prefLabel "How to Think About the Future (Part 1): Changing the Future Starts with How You Think"@en ;
    skos:definition "Opens the series by naming a pattern Hagens says he hears repeatedly from the audience: people who have already settled on one storyline about the future and filter everything else through it. Introduces Scenario Thinking as the alternative, names 3 reasons holding multiple futures at once is hard in practice (the nervous system wants resolution, careers/identities attach to particular futures, cultural incentives reward confident stories over honest uncertainty), and closes by introducing Shortfall Risk, which the episode's own description states will act as connective tissue across the rest of the series."@en ;
    dct:created "2026-04-11"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:issued "2026-04-17"^^xsd:date ;
    dct:subject tgs:Subject.HumanBehavior, tgs:Subject.SystemsScience ;
    thinkr:hasHost tgs:Persona.NateHagens ;
